#X text 202 143 8th notes \, e.g. 6/8 = 2 groups of 3;
#X text 202 173 16th notes \, e.g. 15/16 = 3 groups of 5;
#X text 202 113 common 4-based meters: 4/4 \, 3/4 \, 4/4 triplets;
#X msg 112 363 90;
#X msg 232 363 120;
#X msg 32 363 60;
#X msg 72 363 80;
#X msg 152 363 100;
#X msg 192 363 110;
#X msg 312 363 160;
#X msg 272 363 140;
#X text 202 203 You can also use even more exotic time signatures such
as 12/12 (which is effectively the same as 4 4 3) and 11/9 (= 11 3
3). CAVEAT: Note that \, due to rounding issues \, denominators such
as 9 that do *not* divide 96 = 3*2^5 evenly \, will not work well with
MIDI clock sync \, resulting in a tempo which is slightly off. (Raptor's
internal time base will work alright with these \, though.), f 52
;
#X text 22 23 Raptor lets you use pretty much any time signature whatsoever
\, and will produce convincing rhythms for almost any of them. Time
signatures are specified as pairs n m \, or as triples n m d \, where
d denotes a subdivision of the base pulse. (E.g. \, d=2 denotes duplets
\, d=3 triplets \, d=5 quintuplets etc.), f 79;
